48. Fiscal Federalism and Centre–State Financial Relations in India
Paper: Public Finance (ECNCC303) | Unit: VI | Topic: Fiscal federalism: principles of federal finance, inter-government resource transfer, advantages and limitations of decentralisation; Centre-State financial relations in India
Introduction
Fiscal federalism studies the division of taxing, spending and borrowing powers among levels of government. Its pioneers are Musgrave (1959) and Oates (1972).
Principles of federal finance
1. Musgrave: the central government should handle stabilisation and distribution; allocation of local public goods can be decentralised.
2. Oates' decentralisation theorem: where benefits are local and costs of provision are the same, local provision is more efficient, since it matches local preferences.
3. Subsidiarity: decisions at the lowest competent level.
4. Tax assignment: mobile bases and progressive taxes with the Centre; immobile bases (property) with local government.
5. Equivalence principle (Olson) and fiscal equivalence.
6. Fiscal autonomy and accountability: matching powers and responsibilities.
Inter-governmental resource transfers
Reasons: vertical imbalance (the Centre has more revenue capacity), horizontal imbalance (regional disparities), spillovers and national minimum standards. Types: unconditional (general-purpose) grants, conditional (specific purpose) grants, matching and non-matching, tax-sharing. Effects: flypaper effect (money sticks where it lands), soft budget constraint.
Advantages of decentralisation
Better matching of preferences, local knowledge, accountability and participation, competition and innovation (Tiebout), flexibility.
Limitations
Spillovers and economies of scale lost, regional inequality, administrative capacity gaps, race to the bottom in taxes, macro-stabilisation difficulties and borrowing risks.
Centre-State financial relations in India: overview
• Constitution: Seventh Schedule's Union, State and Concurrent Lists; Articles 268-293. Centre has major revenue sources (income tax, corporation tax, customs) while states carry large expenditure responsibilities, causing vertical imbalance.
• Finance Commission (Article 280): appointed every five years; recommends the sharing of central taxes (the 15th FC: 41 per cent devolution; 14th: 42 per cent), grants-in-aid and local-body grants. The 16th Finance Commission's award covers the 2026-31 period (check its report for the latest devolution share).
• Planning Commission (abolished 2014) gave plan transfers; replaced by NITI Aayog; centrally sponsored schemes continue.
• GST (2017): unified indirect taxes, GST Council as a federal forum, compensation to states ended in 2022; states lost some autonomy.
• Borrowing: Article 293 limits state borrowing; FRBM at state level.
• Issues: cesses and surcharges outside the divisible pool, rising centrally sponsored schemes, disparity among states, and demands for greater autonomy.
Conclusion
Fiscal federalism balances efficiency and equity. India's system is moving toward cooperative federalism, but the challenges of vertical and horizontal imbalances persist.